<p>A brand video for a nonprofit or school should be planned as a set of assets, not a single video. Shoot one strong story about who you are and why it matters, then cut it into versions for every place your audience meets you: a full film for fundraising events and donor meetings, short clips for social media, a looping piece for your website and segments that welcome new families, staff and volunteers.</p>

<p>The difference between a video that plays once at a gala and one that works all year comes down to planning before anyone hits record. Here's how to get the most out of one shoot, whether you run a school in Albuquerque, a nonprofit in Santa Fe or a community group anywhere in New Mexico.</p>

<h2>Why one well planned film beats several small videos</h2>

<p>Most nonprofits and schools don't have the budget or staff time to produce a new video for every campaign. That's exactly why one well planned brand film makes sense. A single shoot with a clear story gives you footage you can keep using across channels, and every version reinforces the same message, so donors, parents and volunteers hear one consistent story wherever they find you.</p>

<p>For Chesterton Academy of Albuquerque, DYAD produced a five minute brand film in two weeks, built from the start for reuse across fundraising, social media, the website and onboarding. You can <a href="/immerse/chesterton-brand-film.mp4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">watch the Chesterton Academy brand film here</a>.</p>

<h2>Start with the story, not the shot list</h2>

<p>Before you think about cameras, decide what one thing you want people to feel and remember. For most schools and nonprofits it's some version of "this is who we are, this is the difference we make, and here's how you can be part of it."</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pick one core message.</strong> If you try to cover every program, the video won't say anything clearly.</li>
  <li><strong>Show real people.</strong> Students, teachers, families, volunteers and the people you serve are more convincing than any script.</li>
  <li><strong>Film real moments.</strong> A rehearsal, a class discussion, a service day or a busy morning drop off says more than a staged hallway walk.</li>
  <li><strong>End with an invitation.</strong> Give, enroll, volunteer, visit. The full film and each cut should point somewhere.</li>
</ul>

<h2>List every place the video will live</h2>

<p>This is the step most organizations skip, and it's the one that decides whether the footage gets reused. Write down every place you'd like the video to show up, then plan for each one before the shoot.</p>

<h3>Fundraising</h3>

<p>The full film is your centerpiece for galas, donor meetings, grant presentations and year end appeals. Donors want to see the impact of their gift, so make sure the story shows outcomes and real faces, not just facilities. A shorter version that works in an email or on a giving page helps too, since not every donor will sit through the whole film.</p>

<h3>Social media</h3>

<p>Short clips pulled from the same footage become posts, Reels and Stories for weeks or months. One strong quote, one emotional moment or one quick behind the scenes look can each stand alone. Since many people scroll with the sound off, add captions and make sure the first few seconds grab attention on their own. If you want to build a steady posting rhythm around that footage, our post on <a href="/blog/grow-instagram-local-business">growing on Instagram without paid ads</a> walks through it.</p>

<h3>Your website</h3>

<p>A brand film can anchor your homepage, about page or admissions page. Put the full film where people are deciding whether to get involved, and consider a short silent loop for the top of the page. Make sure it's compressed so it loads quickly on a phone. If your site needs more than a new video, our <a href="/blog/website-redesign-checklist-new-mexico">website redesign checklist</a> covers the bigger picture.</p>

<h3>Onboarding</h3>

<p>This one gets overlooked. The same film that convinces a donor can welcome a new family, teacher, staff member or volunteer. It tells them what the organization values in a few minutes, in a way a handbook can't. Plan a section or cut that speaks directly to newcomers, so it can play at orientation or go out in a welcome email.</p>

<h2>Plan the shoot for reuse</h2>

<p>Once you know where the video will live, you can capture what each version needs in one go.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Shoot more than you need.</strong> Extra b-roll of classrooms, events and everyday moments gives you material for clips long after the film is done.</li>
  <li><strong>Think about vertical.</strong> Social platforms favor vertical video. Framing key moments so they can be cropped to vertical, or filming some of them vertically, saves a lot of trouble later.</li>
  <li><strong>Record clean audio for interviews.</strong> Strong soundbites can become standalone quote clips, captions or pull quotes in print.</li>
  <li><strong>Grab still photos too.</strong> Real images from the same day work on your website, in newsletters and in grant reports.</li>
  <li><strong>Get permissions up front.</strong> Schools especially should confirm photo and video releases for students before filming, and know who can and can't appear on camera.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A simple reuse plan for one brand film</h2>

<p>Here's a practical way to map one shoot into a year of use:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Full brand film.</strong> Fundraising events, donor meetings, the website and orientation.</li>
  <li><strong>Short version.</strong> Email appeals, giving pages and presentations where time is tight.</li>
  <li><strong>Social clips.</strong> A series of short, captioned moments released over several weeks or months.</li>
  <li><strong>Website loop.</strong> A short, silent piece that sets the tone at the top of a page.</li>
  <li><strong>Onboarding cut.</strong> A welcome version for new families, staff or volunteers.</li>
  <li><strong>Stills.</strong> Photos for print, newsletters, reports and your Google Business Profile.</li>
</ol>

<p>You don't have to release everything at once. Spacing clips out keeps your channels active and gives the film a longer life.</p>

<h2>Mistakes that limit a brand video</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Making it only for one event.</strong> If the film is built entirely around one gala, it will feel dated by the next one.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaning on dates and one time details.</strong> Keep the core film evergreen and save timely messages for short clips.</li>
  <li><strong>Talking heads only.</strong> Interviews matter, but people remember what they see. Cut to real moments often.</li>
  <li><strong>No captions.</strong> Captions help people who watch without sound and make the video more accessible.</li>
  <li><strong>Letting it sit in a folder.</strong> Decide who will post the clips and when, so the footage actually gets used.</li>
</ul>

